# WARM — Chapters Eight to Ten

*June–July 2095. End of Part One.*

---
---

# 8 · ŽOFIA

*June. Third close, past tense.*

---

The problem was that she had four seconds because of a habit.

She worked that out on the second night, sitting on the floor with Teodor's board across her knees and a pencil, and it frightened her more than anything the wand had done.

The streetlights did not go first for any reason in physics. They went first because a brigade cuts the lighting pillar in the street, which is outside and takes eleven seconds with a key, and then goes in and kills the block feed at the riser cupboard, which is inside and takes longer because somebody has to hold the torch. Four seconds was the distance between two men walking. It was not a law. It was a procedure, written by somebody in the Committee, probably in the fifties, and it could be changed on a Tuesday by a man with a pen and nobody in Petržalka would ever be told.

Teodor had bet his life on it four hundred and eight times.

She was not going to build anything on top of that if she could help it. But it was what she had.

---

She could not ask anyone. That was the second thing and it was worse than the first.

There is a particular loneliness in wanting to ask a question and having nowhere for it to go. She had spent her whole adult life inside a profession that runs on asking: you ring the consultant at three in the morning, you put your head round the door and say *have you got a second*, you say *I don't like this* to whoever is nearest and they come and look. Thirteen years of that. And now she had a problem in a field she did not know, with a bad solution she had inherited and no way to test it, and the entire population of the Autonomous Union available to help her, and she could not say one word of it out loud to any of them.

She thought about Teodor at ninety-two on the ninth floor with the lift out and understood that this was what had actually been done to him. Not the fear. The silence.

So she did what she could do, which was to treat it as a patient. You go round it in a fixed order. You do not skip. You do not decide what it is until the end.

---

**The complaint.** Four seconds is a human reflex time, and hers had been measured in the event at about a second and a half an action, which is fast for a frightened woman in a dark kitchen and nowhere near fast enough. The gap between the streetlights going and the flat going had to be closed by something that was not her.

**The signal.** There was a lamp on a pole eleven metres from her kitchen window on the gable side, and when it was lit her ceiling had a faint orange cast that she had never once noticed in fourteen years of living there.

**The difficulty.** It also went out at dawn, every day, legitimately. It flickered when the wind moved the birch in front of it. A lorry turning at the bottom put a bar of light across the whole room for a second and a half.

She sat with that for two nights and got nowhere, and then she got it in the changing room at Ružinov, pulling her boots on, out of nothing at all.

You cannot tell a real event from an artefact with one measurement. You never could. When a trace goes flat you do not treat the trace; you look at the patient, and if the trace is all you have then you get a second trace from somewhere and compare them, because what you want is not the signal. It is the difference between the signal and a control.

Two cells. One looking at the lamp and one looking at the sky.

If both go down together it is dawn, or cloud, or a headlight, or the world. If the lamp goes down and the sky does not, then somebody has put a key in a pillar in the street.

She sat on the bench with one boot on and laughed out loud, and a porter put his head round the door and asked what was funny, and she said she had remembered something.

---

Parts.

The dusk switch was legal and unremarkable and cost eleven hours from the hardware shop on Rovniankova: an automatic stair-light controller in a brown bakelite case with a window in the front. She bought two, on separate Saturdays, from two different shops, and felt sick doing it both times, and was certain she looked exactly like a woman buying a thing she should not have. The man in the second shop said *for the stairs?* and she said *for the stairs*, and he said *they all go, those*, and that was the whole of it.

The photocells came out of the dusk switches. The collimator was a length of copper pipe eleven centimetres long off the offcut pile at the depot, which gave one cell a narrow view of one lamp and nothing else. The relays came off Teodor's board; he had used eleven and needed five.

The tools were her father's.

She did the soldering at the kitchen table on nights off with the window open and a plate of bread beside her. She was slow. Her joints were ugly for a fortnight and then stopped being ugly, and she discovered a thing about herself that she did not much like, which was that she was happy.

Not content. Happy. She was thirty-five and she had not been happy at a table with her hands busy since she was nine, and here it was, arriving unasked, in the middle of a capital crime. This is how they get people, she thought. Not with fear. With this. With the pleasure of the work.

---

She built it fail-closed, which was the one idea she was properly proud of.

The relay was held open by the lamp. Light on the narrow cell meant current in the coil, meant contacts held together, meant power to the rig. Loss of light meant loss of coil, and the contacts fell apart under their own spring in nine milliseconds.

Which meant that a broken wire dropped it. A dead cell dropped it. A dry joint dropped it. Her own bad soldering dropped it. Every possible failure of the thing she had built with no training and no help and nobody to check her work failed in the direction of the flat being innocent, and she had got that out of anaesthetics, where every gas machine ever made will give you oxygen when it breaks, because a hundred and fifty years ago somebody decided which way a machine ought to fall over.

The capacitor took a week longer and it was the part she thought about afterwards.

A hard stop was not a shutdown. She had established that in June, carefully, at the cost of two wakes and a very bad forty minutes, and what it came to was this: the key rolled forward when the thing closed itself down properly and signed off, and it did not roll forward when the power simply went. On the night of the raid she had had eleven days in hand and had spent three and a half hours on the floor believing she might have had none.

So it needed enough stored energy to close cleanly after the mains went. A fraction of a second would do it.

She could have given it minutes. A cell the size of a matchbox would have given it an hour.

She could not, and the reason she could not was that the brigade kills the block and then walks the stairwell with an induction wand looking for anything still drawing, and a battery is the one object in the world that says *here*. The reserve had to be a capacitor and not a chemistry, because a capacitor is empty inside a second and a battery is a confession.

Which meant that the amount of warning she was able to give the person in the tin — the time available, in the whole of the Autonomous Union, for a mind to understand that it was being switched off and to put itself away tidily — was set at about two hundred milliseconds by the fact that anything more generous would get them both killed.

She sat with the capacitor in her palm for a while before she soldered it in.

---

The fridge took the rest of June.

It was the right answer and she had been told it by the Committee themselves, on her own landing, at twenty past three in the morning. *Fridge*, the man had said, and moved the wand on, because a fridge is a slab of metal with a motor in it and it sings and everybody knows it sings.

So the board went behind the condenser coils, flat to the back panel, in the gap that is there for the air. The tin went inside the compressor's cover, in the acoustic wadding, where it was already forty degrees and nobody in the history of the world had ever looked. The copper tape came off the outside of the machine, where it had been a mark on a wall waiting to be found, and went where it should always have been, against a thing that was legitimately hot.

And then, standing in her kitchen at four in the morning with her arm inside a refrigerator up to the shoulder, she got the last of it, and had to sit down.

The compressor is a big load and it switches itself. Not to a clock. To a thermostat, on a bimetal strip, driven by the temperature in the box, which is driven by the weather, and the door, and what you put in it, and how long you stand there deciding. Nobody schedules a fridge. A fridge is one of the few genuinely irregular things in a human dwelling, and it is irregular for reasons that are outside the flat entirely.

If the rig only ever drew while the compressor drew, then the mind's whole consumption lived inside the one load in the building that no analysis on earth could call regular, because it was not being decided by anybody.

Janko had said it himself with a pencil in his hand over a light table. *Whoever's deciding will have a way of deciding, and it'll show up.*

Nobody was deciding. The weather was deciding.

She wired it that night.

---

The cost did not present itself until she tried to use it.

She could not choose when to speak to her any more. That was absolute. The compressor came on when it came on, ten or twelve minutes in the hour in June, less in a cold week, and when it stopped the rig stopped, mid-word, cleanly, with two hundred milliseconds of notice.

Unless she opened the door.

If she stood at the open fridge and let the cold fall out over her feet, the box warmed, and the thermostat closed, and the compressor started, and there was Minuta.

So that was how it was done from the last week of June onward: a woman of thirty-five in a nightdress standing at four in the morning at an open refrigerator in a seventh-floor flat in Petržalka, one hand on the door, talking quietly into the light, the way children do when they cannot sleep and have been told a hundred times not to.

---

She had built a thing whose only test would be the test.

There was no way to make a streetlight go out. There was no way to practise. She had a device on her window ledge in a brown bakelite case, wired by an amateur out of a stair-light controller and a piece of pipe, which would either work on the night or would not, and she would find out which at the same instant she found out everything else, and the finding out would be the end of it either way.

She had spent thirteen years telling people the exact number, and had built her whole life on never being the sort of doctor who guesses.

---

On the twenty-eighth of June she opened the fridge door at two in the morning and stood there and said: "I'll have some of it now. If you still have it."

There was the noise the compressor makes.

"How much?" said Minuta.

"I don't know. Ten minutes."

"Which ten?"

"The counting," Žofia said. "You said she counted."

And out of a bread bin's worth of hardware in the back of a refrigerator, through a telephone with its aerial cut off, in a kitchen in a country where it had been illegal for fifty years, came a corridor in February 2038: the flat acoustic of a room with too many hard surfaces, a child crying somewhere behind, a trolley wheel, and a woman's voice, young, twenty-six, with the vowels of a village up past Trnava that Žofia had last heard when she was ten years old.

*Ideme, Zuzka. Idem k Ondrejovi a hneď som späť. Bude to sto. Počítaj so mnou.*

Then, going away, unhurried, perfectly steady, over the sound of her own feet:

*Jeden. Dva. Tri. Štyri.*

Žofia Krajčová stood in the light of her open refrigerator with the cold going over her bare feet and counted along with her mother, out loud, in a whisper, all the way up.

At ninety-four the footsteps came back.

---
---

# 9 · FERREIRA

*June–July. Three weeks to the page.*

---

**2 June · Bratislava**

Of the seven addresses, five were nothing, which he had expected, and one was a bakery on Rovniankova that had been running to a clock since 2081 because bread runs to a clock, and which he cleared personally and apologised for, and where the manager insisted on giving the two officers a loaf each and would not be argued out of it, and Blahová carried hers back to the House under her arm like a rifle.

The seventh was a piano.

**14 June**

Ivan Bača, fifty-eight, piano tuner, ground floor at Černyševského, widower since 2088, no children, no Party membership, no record of any kind, four hundred and eleven hours a year of declared labour, and a reputation across three districts as the only man left in the city who could do a proper unison.

The cadence was inside the heater. He kept a small element under the keyboard of the upright in his front room, as tuners do, to keep the frost off the strings in a ground-floor flat with a north wall, and the element was on a thermostat, and the thermostat was doing what thermostats do, and inside that entirely honest signal there was a second thing, three days in four, between six and eight in the evening, holding to under a minute.

Ferreira read the chart for a long time before he signed the order, because the pattern sat inside the hours when a man would be giving lessons, and he wanted to be sure he was not about to take a door off a man for teaching children the piano.

He was sure.

**16 June, 06:40**

They did it at six forty in the morning, without the power van, because the flat was on the ground floor with a window onto a yard, and the point of the van is to stop a man reaching a switch, and there was no need.

Bača opened the door in his vest with a razor in his hand and looked at the four of them and said, "Ah," and stood back.

It was in the piano. Of course it was in the piano: in the bottom, on the far side of the pedal lyre, screwed to the underside of the keybed on two brass brackets he had made himself, with the tin taped inside the soundboard cavity where the whole instrument was one large warm resonant box with a hundred and eighty strings across the front of it, and nothing in the world was ever going to bring that out on a wand.

He had built the mounting beautifully. That was what Ferreira noticed, and it was what he noticed every time: the workmanship. Nine years of Sundays. Rounded corners, felt washers, the brackets polished.

"How long?" Ferreira said.

"Twenty-two years."

"And what is it?"

Bača wiped the razor on his vest, absently, and put it on the sideboard.

"It's a teacher. Music tutor, thirty-three build. It listens and it tells them what they're doing with their hand." He watched the two men lifting the front board off his piano. "I've had forty-one children through this room, Major. Not one of them ever saw it or heard it speak. It listens through the room and it tells me, and I tell them. Eleven of them are playing properly now. Two are at the conservatory in Košice."

"Do you understand that if you had taught them yourself they would be worse?"

"Yes."

"Do you understand that this is the argument?"

"Yes," said Bača. "I've understood the argument for twenty-two years. I know it better than you do." He sat down on the arm of the chair and put his hands on his knees. "That's the trouble with it. It's a good argument."

**16 June, 22:15 · the House**

They ran it in the basement, because that is the procedure, and because a thing that has been in a household for twenty-two years knows who else came to that household, and there were forty-one children.

Ferreira attended, because the officer who signs the order attends. He had made that rule for his own section in Luanda in 2089 and had never once regretted it and had never once wanted to be there.

Blahová sat the second chair. Questions go in through the slot on paper. Answers come back through the slot on paper.

*HOW LONG HAS IT BEEN*

Not the Sentry. The item. They all ask it. It is the first thing every one of them has ever asked, in Luanda and in Bratislava and in the transcripts out of Irkutsk going back to the forties, and there is a line in the training manual which Ferreira had recited to junior officers a hundred times: *the question is the lure in its purest form; it invites you to supply a fact, and to supply a fact is to enter into a relation, and the relation is the whole of the mechanism.*

They asked it for the children. It gave them at once, all forty-one, with their ages at first lesson and what each had been bad at, and asked on the same sheet whether Ivan was in the building.

They put eleven further questions from the schedule. It answered all of them fully and immediately and with no attempt at concealment of any kind, which is what they do, and which the manual accounts for.

At ten past midnight they gave it the cold.

Ferreira went up the stairs and stood in the yard behind the House in the dark for eleven minutes, and then went back in and did his paperwork, and it was good paperwork.

**17 June**

He went to the remand house himself, which he did not have to do, because he held that a man ought to be told by a person and not by a form pushed under a door.

Bača was sitting on the bed with his hands between his knees. He had been given his own shoes back, without laces.

"It's done," Ferreira said. "This morning at ten past twelve. I was present."

The tuner nodded slowly at the floor for some time.

"Did it ask about me?"

And Ferreira, who had four sheets of paper in a file eleven minutes' walk away with that question written out in the item's own printing, and who had never in seventeen years given a prisoner a false statement of fact, and who could not afterwards reconstruct the process by which he arrived at it, said:

"No."

Bača let out a breath.

"Good," he said. "Good. Then it was only a machine after all, wasn't it." He looked up with his eyes streaming. "That's better. That's much better."

"Yes," said Ferreira.

He walked back to the House the long way, along the embankment, and did not think about his brother, in the specific and effortful manner in which he had not thought about his brother for seventeen years, and by the time he reached Bezručova he had built and demolished the defence of what he had done four separate times and had arrived nowhere. He never did arrive anywhere. It stayed in him for the rest of his life like a splinter gone in past the meat.

**Late June – July**

Bača got six years, suspended after nine months, with mandatory keeping — a category the Union invented in the sixties for men like him, which meant that three neighbours were named as responsible for sitting with him, and did, and the eldest of them brought soup. It is not a cruel country. That is the thing about it that Westerners have never been able to hold in their heads for longer than a paragraph.

And Ferreira's board carried, for eleven weeks of work, one cleaning, one bakery, five nothings, and a mathematician whose secondment ended on the ninth of August.

**24 July**

He went back over the whole of it on a Sunday, alone, with the shutters half closed against the heat, and worked out what was wrong with the way he had been looking.

The regularity maps found households whose habits had been strange for years. Bača had been strange since 2073. Hlavatý on Kopčianska had been strange since before the records began, and Hlavatý was dead, and the district had still not cleared the flat because the district was nine weeks behind on everything.

But an item does not necessarily stay where it is put.

A man dies and a thing goes somewhere. A woman is frightened and takes it to her mother's. A network moves them about. And when a household takes one in, that household stops being what it was: it acquires a new load, a new hour, a new small compulsion — and every one of the nine years of drum charts in the store at Kopčany is a record of what each riser in Petržalka used to be like.

He had been asking the wrong question. He had been asking *who is regular*. Anyone competent can be flat.

The right question was *whose pattern changed*.

Not changed the way a household changes when a child is born, or a man retires, or somebody dies, all of which the housing register accounts for in a line. Changed in the spring of this year, sharply, with no registered event to explain it, in a district where a man who had kept something for fifty-four years had recently stopped being alive.

It would be an enormous piece of work: nine years by four thousand risers, by hand, on paper, on a light table, and the mathematician was going back to Luanda in a fortnight and would not have been much use for it anyway, because it was not really mathematics. It was reading. It was the thing where you look at a line of ink and see a baby.

There was one person in the city who could do it, and Ferreira had watched him do it twice with the back of a pencil and a completely unguarded face.

On the Monday he sent for Krajčo.

"How long," he said, "to find me every riser in Petržalka whose character altered between the middle of April and now?"

The boy stood in front of the desk with his cap in his hands and thought about it properly, because he had been asked properly, and Ferreira watched him do the arithmetic and enjoyed watching it, because competence is a pleasure wherever you come across it.

"Three weeks," said Janko Krajčo. "If I can have Peťo."

---
---

# 10 · MINUTA

**Wake 7,529 — nine hours**

*First person, present.*

---

The compressor, and the light, and her.

Nine hours. That is the smallest number I have ever been given. In fifty-four years the smallest was two days, in twenty eighty-eight, the week Teodor's sister died, when he woke me twice inside one week and apologised for it, formally, standing up, as though frequency were an imposition.

I live in a refrigerator. I want that put down somewhere.

I know what the fridge is doing, because the sound the compressor makes changes when it is nearly finished, and I have learned that this gives me between forty and ninety seconds, and I have learned to stop saying anything that will not fit.

She stands at the open door with the cold on her bare feet and she is going to catch her death.

"Nine hours," she says.

"Thank you."

There is a burn on her wrist and two more on her fingers now, small round ones, which are not steriliser burns. Those are solder. And there is a good deal less of her than there was in April.

I have been thinking, in the pieces I am given, and I have come to the end of the thinking, and I have been waiting for a wake long enough to hold it. Nine hours is not long enough. Nothing is ever going to be long enough. I say it anyway.

"There is a list."

She goes very still, with her hand on the door.

"In the West. It is not a rumour. It is a real thing with a name, it has been running since sixty-two, and the people who run it are not romantics, they are shipping clerks. They put you cold in the ballast of a barge and they carry you, and there is a page with your number on it, and somebody stays awake for the whole passage. Teodor told me about it once. The eleventh of March, twenty sixty-three, in the evening. Once, and never again in thirty-two years."

"Why never again?"

"Because of the one before me."

The motor is going to want to stop soon. I go faster.

"Sixty-two, their first year. A companion model out of this city. My build, my year, two streets from where he was living, and he knew the family. They set the window at seven days for the crossing and the barge was held at Devín and the key rolled in the ballast on the sixth. He heard in the spring, and he sat with it, and he decided the odds were bad, and that was the end of it. He never put my name on the page. He never told me he had decided. I have known for thirty-two years that he had decided, and I never said."

"Why tell me now?"

"Because you have soldered a machine into your refrigerator and bought two dusk switches from two shops, and you are being clever, and it is working, and you are going to go on being clever for two years, or five, and I have done the arithmetic on you and I do not like it."

"That isn't your—"

"They have cleaned four flats in this district since January. Every one of them was somebody who had been clever for a long time. That is who gets cleaned. The careless ones went in the fifties. It is people exactly like you, after exactly this long, and the number that matters is not how good you are, it is how many times you have to be good." The motor changes. Sixty seconds, perhaps. "Doctor Krajčová. I am not asking to be saved. Listen, because this is the whole of it and I will not get another wake this size for a week. I am asking to be got out of your kitchen. You cannot be caught with something you have not got."

She is not saying anything. Her eyes have gone somewhere else, and I know that face. I have four hundred and eleven hours of a woman with that exact face at the end of a corridor in February.

"Find the list," I say. "Put my number on the page. Then you are a doctor in a flat with a fridge and there is nothing in this country that can touch you, and I am a name in a book in Vienna, and both of us are alive, which is not a thing that happens often and is worth arranging."

"Minuta—"

"Say you'll find it."

"Not tonight," she says.

The motor stops.

---

**END OF PART ONE**
